Run the following commands sequentially to install Jenkins on your Ubuntu system.

***********Use code with caution********************************

1. Install Java PrerequisitesJenkins is built on Java and requires it to run. Update your system and install OpenJDK 21 (or OpenJDK 17):

sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y fontconfig openjdk-21-jre



2. Add the Official Jenkins RepositoryThe default Ubuntu repository packages are often outdated. Download the official Jenkins GPG signing key and add the stable repository to your package manager sources:bash

# Create directory for the keyring
sudo mkdir -p /etc/apt/keyrings

# Download and add the signing key
sudo wget -O /etc/apt/keyrings/jenkins-keyring.asc https://pkg.jenkins.io/debian-stable/jenkins.io-2026.key

# Register the Jenkins repository
echo "deb [signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/jenkins-keyring.asc] https://pkg.jenkins.io/debian-stable binary/" | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/jenkins.list > /dev/null

Use code with caution.

3. Install JenkinsRefresh your system's package list to recognize the new repository database and install Jenkins:bash

sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y jenkins
Use code with caution.

4. Manage the Jenkins ServiceStart the automation server and set it to launch automatically whenever your system boots:bashsudo systemctl start jenkins
sudo systemctl enable Jenkins

Use code with caution.

To confirm that the service started successfully, run:bash
sudo systemctl status Jenkins


5. Access JenkinsIf your firewall is active, allow traffic through port 8080 (the default port for Jenkins):bash
sudo ufw allow 8080


